HAAND EEEEEEEYE..

@Joe

Yes, taking @Lewis' perspective (which he shares with us pretty much daily-!) is one to aspire to for sure. Consider it a mentorship of sorts — I certainly do! Lewis is a yoga instructor and a NYT-published crossword constructor, so it's coming from a good place! 

Mind you, I don't believe I've ever read a single negative expression from him, and I certainly am not as rosy. But I do take a moment and review before hitting Submit: is there a more constructive way to voice my disapproval, or can I reword, or even is that gripe worth it? For me, I feel a lot better as a result! Less combative, a tiny bit more Zen.  :)

@SP

Anyone whose given name is a Mc or Mac name is called Mac(k) for short. 

Macaulay Culkin, Mackenzie Phillips, etc.

@RinF
What I meant was, that in the UK, BEAU definitely does not come naturally as a name, and I only have the vaguest memory of someone called Beau Bridges. Film stars are not my forte. 

Ty/Tyson is not common either, and I've never watched Modern Family or a single game of basketball. Likewise, MAC is definitely never used as a name, and I'm a Scot! I would have noticed.

@lucky13
No, no ATM (or cash machine) fees anywhere. ARBOR day sounds like a wonderful idea.

Unfortunately, I'm hopeless with film stars, and I know of not a single basketball player except Jordan and that's because of the shoes.

@SP
Thank you! 
I'm always interested (in a non-complaining way) in differences in customs and language use between cultures.

@Grant

When I was a kid, there was a MATRON on Saturdays in the movie theatre. All the children would have to sit in the back on the left side of the theatre and the MATRON, always dressed in a white uniform, would keep us in line.

Of course, that was when a lot of kids were sent to the movies by themselves on a Saturday morning.

@Jane Wheelaghan

Beau is usually a Southern name, originally short for Beauregard. 

Ty can be short for Tyson, Tyrone, Tyler, etc.

@Janet

This is the place for comments on all three daily crosswords. 

I don't play the Midi, but will today. Thanks for the TIP about the constructor: Paulo Pasco is one of the greats!

@Michael G

Yes, the x was probably the biggest misdirect of all! My first thought at 17A was that perhaps it was a multiplier. The first themer I filled in was MACADAMIA, so I counted repeated letters. But no, 2 M's and 4 A's... 

After I got the revealer, I could see the final name in each theme entry — but it took reading the column  to see the rest. When I reached the constructor's notes, I took one glance at the bonus entries, and took a pass! For me, the theme only worked in retrospect. Sussing out the words without the benefit of crossers seems like a game for a *really* rainy day. Like tomorrow's forecast!

@Emma

Did you dislike the ATM FEE clue because it was a poor clue, or because you didn’t get it?

By my measure, it was a delightfully vague and clever clue.

@Jane Wheelaghan

At least in the US, if you use the ATM of a bank other than your own, you’re charged an ATM FEE by that bank. And sometimes also by your own bank. 

I have heard that Brits call ATMs “cashpoints,” which is only very occasionally seen here.

@Nancy
I did feel like if you are going to do this puzzle you ought to try to do it with more standard names and spellings. But not having tried this myself I imagine it’s harder than it looks, and I just appreciated the creativity and the sheer chutzpah of it all. I for one loved “EDUMACATE”. 

I also wanted to add, I work in a low income community clinic. The creativity and diversity of names there is refreshing and fascinating—I love asking parents how they came up with them. So in my book CARA and UMA and CATE are pretty darn standard in the grand scheme of things.

@Captain Quahog

That sounds remarkable.  

I love stories where science thinks one thing, and it turns out to be the complete opposite.  The ability for life to "find a way" is truly breathtaking.

@oceans are awesome - CEOAS didn't even exist back then -- I had to google it to see what you meant! 

To give some perspective, I was on one of the very first short research cruises on R/V Wecoma in 1976. It really bounced around in the Pacific swells! We were coring the tops of seamounts off the PNW coast to sample long-undisturbed sediments that provided a record of the eruptions of the Cascade volcanoes. Fun even for this young biologist working for the geologists! 

What was your research area? I studied the symbiosis between the sulfide-oxidizing bacteria and the giant tube worms and the vent muscles, as well as some aspects of sulfide toxicity in the vent crabs.

@Crevecoeur -- Then your daughter was dealing rather directly with the MAGMA that appears as an entry in this puzzle! 

Cool! 


Oh, wait... Hot stuff!

@Charles Peterson - Nice catch, my fellow zoologist!

I hate autocorrect. 

Orange yew grapefruit fur spelt Czech?

@Charles
That's charlesplaining, man.

I should know.

@Jim
And they sort of miss the central quality of ESP:  it is not something one turns on at will.  
I cannot explain why there are people to whom I am sensitive...and it can be uncomfortable.  Once our son said to me, "Mom, I wish you would stop doing that."  ...as if it was by my own volition.  Other individuals were surprised but not bothered...
